Don't spin in SDL_WaitEvent() on Android.

On Android, SDL_WaitEventTimeoutNS() loops pumping events (SDL_PumpEventsInternal(true)) and
waiting in Android_PumpEvents() for a lifecycle event. Pumping pushes a poll sentinel, and
pushing any event sends a wakeup (SDL_ANDROID_LIFECYCLE_WAKE), so the wait ended at once every
time: with nothing to do, the thread spun through the loop about a million times a second, a
whole CPU core busy (SDL_HINT_MAIN_CALLBACK_RATE "waitevent" runs every idle moment there).

The loop pumps without a sentinel now. The sentinel only marks the end of a poll cycle for
SDL_PollEvent(); the wait looks for real events with SDL_PeepEvents(), which never returns a
sentinel, and any real event still sends the wakeup that ends the wait.

Kompoz applies this to SDL 3.4.18 (scripts/fetch-deps.sh).

--- a/src/events/SDL_events.c	2026-09-02 01:42:43.000000000 +0300
+++ b/src/events/SDL_events.c	2026-10-28 05:55:56.048021131 +0300
@@ -1737,7 +1737,9 @@
 
 #ifdef SDL_PLATFORM_ANDROID
     for (;;) {
-        SDL_PumpEventsInternal(true);
+        /* No poll sentinel here: pushing one sends a wakeup, which ends Android_PumpEvents()'s
+         * wait at once, so the thread would spin through this loop instead of waiting. */
+        SDL_PumpEventsInternal(false);
 
         if (SDL_PeepEvents(event, 1, SDL_GETEVENT, SDL_EVENT_FIRST, SDL_EVENT_LAST) > 0) {
             return true;
